# Files excluded from the published package (`packages/anycodec`).
#
# This file is **required**, not optional: `dart pub publish` falls back to
# `.gitignore` when `.pubignore` is absent, and the repository's `.gitignore`
# describes the whole monorepo rather than this package. Asking "what would be
# shipped?" is then answered by the wrong file, silently.
#
# The patterns below are relative to *this* package, so anything outside
# `packages/anycodec/` — the workspace root's `pubspec.lock`, `.github/`, the
# sibling packages — is never in the archive to begin with. `dart pub publish`
# additionally drops every dot-entry on its own, which is why `.github/` needs no
# rule here.
#
# Every pattern is rooted with a leading `/`. That is deliberate and it is the
# single most expensive lesson in this project's history: an unanchored `build/`
# matches at *any* depth, so it silently deletes `lib/src/build/`, and an
# unanchored `wasmtime/` once deleted a whole `lib/src/wasmtime/` API layer. The
# package published fine and did not compile for anyone. Written as `/build/`,
# the same pattern only ever matches the package root.
#
# `.github/scripts/check_archive.dart` asserts the consequence of getting this
# wrong: it resolves every relative import in the dry-run archive and fails if any
# published library is incomplete.

# --- build output and caches ------------------------------------------------
/.dart_tool/
/build/
/coverage/
/doc/api/
/rust/target/
/example/.dart_tool/
/example/build/
/example/coverage/
/example/.flutter-plugins
/example/.flutter-plugins-dependencies
/example/linux/flutter/ephemeral/
/example/macos/Flutter/ephemeral/
/example/windows/flutter/ephemeral/
/example/ios/Flutter/ephemeral/

# --- editor and OS noise ----------------------------------------------------
/.vscode/
/.idea/
**/*.iml
.DS_Store

# --- lockfiles ---------------------------------------------------------------
# A Dart library must not pin its consumers, so pubspec locks stay out. There is
# no lock in this package anyway: the workspace keeps exactly one, at the
# repository root, which is outside the archive.
# The Rust lock is the opposite case — the build hook compiles a cdylib whose
# dependency versions must not drift under a consumer's feet — so it ships.
/pubspec.lock
/example/pubspec.lock
